Number of subsequences of size k from an array of size n is nCk.

Abstract: nCk and nPk

nCk (Combinations):
The number of ways to choose k items from n total items when order does not matter.
Often read as "n choose k" and written as C(n, k) or nCk.

nPk (Permutations):
The number of ways to choose and arrange k items from n total items when order does matter.
Written as P(n, k) or nPk.


Important fixed rules to remember

For distributing identical objects:
Condition                                           Formula
n children, m identical objects, child can get 0+   C(n + m - 1, m)
Every child gets at least 1                         C(m - 1, n - 1)


DIGITSUM + NO CARRY RULE

    ADDITION:
    digitsum(a) + digitsum(b) = digitsum(a + b)
        ⇔
    There are NO carries when adding a and b.

    For multiple numbers:
    digitsum(a) + digitsum(b) + digitsum(c)
        = digitsum(a + b + c)
        ⇔
    No carries during the addition.


    MULTIPLICATION:
    digitsum(k * n) = k * digitsum(n)
        ⇔
    Multiplying n by k causes NO carries.

    For every digit d of n:
        k * d <= 9

    So:
        d <= floor(9 / k)